          <div class="tm-body">
            <div class="tm-pass">
              <div class="tm-pass-in">
                <h4>Passage 1 (Questions 1–4)</h4>
                <p>CTx belongs to the AB₅ family of toxins: a single enzymatically active A subunit sits on a ring of five identical B subunits, each a small protein of about 11 kDa. Each B subunit can bind the sugar head group of <span class="tm-hl" data-step="1">the ganglioside GM1, a lipid built on ceramide</span> that is concentrated in cholesterol-rich, ordered regions (lipid rafts) of the plasma membrane, so one toxin can gather up to five GM1 molecules together. After binding, the toxin enters the cell by endocytosis and travels backward through the secretory pathway, from endosomes to the endoplasmic reticulum (ER). In the ER, the A subunit separates from the B ring, is unfolded by protein disulfide isomerase, and crosses the ER membrane into the cytosol, where it refolds.</p>
                <p>In the cytosol, the A subunit ADP-ribosylates Gαs, the α subunit of the stimulatory G protein (Figure 1). Because the A subunit is an enzyme, each molecule that reaches the cytosol can modify many Gαs proteins. Modified Gαs keeps adenylyl cyclase active, and the resulting rise in cAMP opens CFTR, a chloride channel in the apical membrane, while inhibiting NHE3, an exchanger that normally absorbs Na⁺ from the lumen.</p>
                <figure>
                  <img src={passageFig.src} width="640" height="330" alt="Pathway diagram of cholera toxin entering an intestinal epithelial cell and raising cAMP" />
                  <figcaption><b>Figure 1</b> Entry and action of cholera toxin in an intestinal epithelial cell</figcaption>
                </figure>
              </div>
              <i class="tm-scroll" data-step="2"></i>
            </div>
            <div class="tm-q">
              <h4>Question 1</h4>
              <p>Based on the passage’s description of GM1, it is best classified as a:</p>
              <ol class="tm-ch">
                <li class="x" data-step="3"><span class="t">A. glycerophospholipid.</span></li>
                <li class="ans" data-step="4"><span class="t">B. sphingolipid.</span></li>
                <li class="x" data-step="3"><span class="t">C. steroid.</span></li>
                <li class="x" data-step="3"><span class="t">D. triacylglycerol.</span></li>
              </ol>
            </div>
          </div>
